    "created_at": "2025-10-02T16:30:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-10-02T16:30:21Z",
    "body": "> This was a rather intentional design decision as upgrade is somewhat costly and we wanted to give web developers control over when that happens. \n\nI agree that control over upgrades would be valuable, but as exposed here it can't be relied upon. I don't think this is critical to change, but it does seem like a foot gun for the common case. In the example above `document.body.append(host)` will also cause the `child` to upgrade. So as an element author, even if I want to keep my initialized tree in an un-upgraded state, I cannot.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
